# Three.js build cache — build.py downloads these on demand, and the same code
# is already inlined into the committed flamme-retarde.html.
vendor/

# Regenerable geodata. tools/fetch_dem.py and tools/fetch_osm.py pull these
# from AWS Terrarium and Overpass; tools/bake.py turns them into build/payload,
# which *is* committed so the game builds without a network round trip.
build/dem.npy
build/dem_meta.json
build/osm/
build/*_preview.png
build/barrier_debug.png

# The MakeHuman base and the working blends it turns into. The small rigid-part
# blends next to them *are* committed, but these are 24, 42 and 46 MB and are
# rewritten by every full run of tools/blender/human_mh.py, which rebuilds them
# from the .obj it fetches. What has to survive is the exported blob in
# build/payload, and that is committed.
#
# chloe_mh.blend is named here because it is NOT covered by the two above and
# was tracked for one release before anyone noticed: it arrived in 1.144.0 with
# the second figure, under a new name, and a rule that lists filenames does not
# see a new filename. Two rebakes put 96 MB of blob in the history. Untracked
# from 1.148.0 — the history still carries what it carries, and that is a
# separate decision because this repository is public.
#
# baye2.blend is the fourth name on this list and it got here the same way
# chloe_mh.blend did — a new figure, a new filename, and a rule that lists
# filenames does not see a new filename. It was tracked for exactly one
# commit (1.468.0, 2.7 MB) before the pattern below replaced the list.
build/mh_base.obj
build/*.blend

# The morph targets and the bodies they make, for the same reason and by the
# same rule as the base above: 8 MB of .target files that tools/blender/
# mh_morph.py fetches on demand, and 15 MB of .obj it writes from them in about
# a second. What survives is build/payload/bather_*.fr3d.gz.
build/mh_targets/
build/mh_bodies/

# Screenshots and scratch output from tools/shoot.mjs
shots/
*.png
!docs/*.png
!build/payload/*.png
!refs/panels/*.png
# The two detail maps the wardrobe viewer puts over a diffuse-only skin. The
# community skins are photographic colour and nothing else, so pores and the
# roughness break-up are ours; they are the one procedural thing from that
# evening worth keeping.
!tools/wardrobe/maps/*.png

# The reference photographs are other people's work — Wikimedia contributors,
# press and stock libraries — gathered locally to model from. They are not ours
# to redistribute, so they stay out of the public repository. tools/gen_panels.py
# needs them only to regenerate the painted panels, and those are committed.
refs/*
!refs/panels/
!refs/README.md
*.blend1

# Python bytecode
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Burst instance state — live rented-GPU ids, per BURST_STATE. Never tracked.
build/*.json

# Scratch from a session that is over. `plan/*.md` is the project's design
# record and is cited by name from the source; the `-wip` directories beside it
# were never that — throwaway probe scripts and contact sheets from an
# afternoon's work on one animation, force-added at the time and then left. If
# a working directory turns out to be worth keeping, it earns a .md.
plan/*-wip/

# The asset packs tools/wardrobe/assets.py downloads, and what it fits out of
# them. 200 MB of other people's zips and a few megabytes of rewritten .obj,
# all regenerable in one command. What survives is the committed wardrobe.html.
build/mh_assets/
build/wardrobe/
